 @end itemize
 
+@section dovi_compose
+
+Compose the two layers of a dual-layer Dolby Vision stream (profile 7, as on
+Ultra HD Blu-ray) into the picture they encode together.
+
+This filter is experimental: it follows the composer of ETSI GS CCM 001 and
+has not been compared with the output of a Dolby decoder.
+
+It takes two inputs: the base layer, an HDR10 picture, and the enhancement
+layer, the picture that holds what the base layer lacks. The composing
+metadata (the RPU) comes as side data of the frames of either. The base layer
+is mapped by the curves of the RPU, the enhancement layer is brought to the
+size of the base layer, inverse quantized and added to it. With a minimal
+enhancement layer (MEL), whose residual is zero, only the curves are applied.
+
+The output is a BT.2020 PQ picture of 12 or 10 bits without Dolby Vision
+metadata, so that what follows treats it as HDR10. The static HDR10 metadata
+of the base layer is kept (see @option{peak}), and the brightness the RPU gives
+each scene goes on as HDR10+ (see @option{scene}).
+
+Both inputs are @code{yuv420p10}, and the enhancement layer is the size of the
+base layer or half of it in each direction. A base layer in another transfer
+than PQ, layers of 8 bits and an NLQ other than the linear dead zone are
+refused with an error.
+
+A picture of the base layer goes with the picture of the enhancement layer
+that has its timestamp. One without such a picture is composed without a
+residual, and one without an RPU is passed on as it is; a warning tells so.
+
+A file that carries both layers in one track is opened twice, the second time
+through the @code{dovi_split} bitstream filter, which keeps the enhancement
+layer:
+
+@example
+ffmpeg -i INPUT -bsf:v:0 dovi_split=mode=el -i INPUT -filter_complex \
+  "[0:v:0][1:v:0]dovi_compose[v]" -map "[v]" -map 0:a OUTPUT
+@end example
+
+Where the layers are two streams, as in the transport streams of a disc, the
+RPU is in the second:
+
+@example
+ffmpeg -i INPUT -filter_complex "[0:v:0][0:v:1]dovi_compose[v]" -map "[v]" \
+  -map 0:a OUTPUT
+@end example
+
+The filter accepts the following options:
+
+@table @option
+@item bits
+Set the bit depth of the output: 12 (@code{yuv420p12}), the depth of the
+composed signal and the default, or 10 (@code{yuv420p10}), rounded from it.
+
+@item residual
+Add the residual of the enhancement layer. Enabled by default; when disabled,
+the output is the base layer mapped by the curves of the RPU alone.
+
+@item peak
+Set the mastering peak in the static metadata of the output. That metadata is
+the base layer's and describes the base layer, while the composed picture can
+be mastered brighter; a tone mapper that trusts the lower peak clips it.
+
+@table @samp
+@item auto
+Keep the base layer's, except in frames that the curves or the residual
+change and whose RPU declares a higher source peak: these get that peak, and
+lose their content light levels, which were measured on the base layer. The
+metadata of the stream, which an encoder writes, stays the base layer's: a
+filter cannot change it once frames flow. This is the default.
+@item bl
+Keep the base layer's.
+@item @var{peak}
+A peak in cd/m2, set in the frames and in the stream; the content light levels
+are dropped from both.
+@end table
+
+@item scene
+Pass the brightness that the RPU's level 1 metadata gives each scene, the
+maximum and the average of its maxRGB, on as the MaxSCL and average maxRGB of
+HDR10+ dynamic metadata in the frames, so that a tone mapper keeps following
+it. It describes the composed picture. Enabled by default; HDR10+ that the
+base layer carries is replaced.
+@end table
+
 @section drawbox
 
 Draw a colored box on the input image.
